# nadia.gate — success is not the model's to declare

`SPEC.md` §3.1. The prompt already tells the model to run the build and read the output,
and a small model does exactly that and no more: it verifies that the program *builds and
runs*, because nobody wrote down what the right answer is. Measured on the Rust twin — a
working RPN calculator whose own printout read `4 + 4 = 7`, reported as finished.

So the criterion is written down **before** the run, by the model itself, and the run is
judged against it. Three rules are contract rather than taste, and every one of them was
paid for:

- **`checkable: false` is a valid answer** — no invented criterion. "reply with the word
  pong" once became `cargo run -- pong == gnop`.
- **A delimiting quote is not part of the argument.** From `cargo run -- "3 4 + 2 *"` the
  model returns the argument the way the task spelled it; the check would then demand a
  program that accepts a quoted argument, which nobody asked for.
- **A project belongs in the workspace root.** `cargo new <name>` makes a subdirectory the
  check cannot see; the repair prompt NAMES it rather than the check moving into it, which
  would turn work delivered where nobody asked into a passing run.

[AgentEndpoint, RunOptions, runAgent, AgentResult](std/agent.ssc)
[exec, ProcessOptions, ProcessResult](std/process.ssc)
[exists](std/fs.ssc)
[entriesOf, isDirSafe](fsx.ssc)
[env](std/os.ssc)
[jsonValue](std/json.ssc)

```scalascript
val derivePrompt =
  "Set up the acceptance CHECK for this coding task. Reply with ONLY a JSON object, no prose:\n" +
  "{\"checkable\": <true|false>, \"cargo_test\": <true|false>, \"run\": [{\"args\": [\"<one entry PER command-line argument>\"], \"expect\": \"<exact stdout>\"}]}\n" +
  "- \"run\": one entry per concrete example the task states as `cargo run -- X` printing `Y`.\n" +
  "  `args` is the argument LIST X, one entry per argument; `expect` is JUST Y. Omit if no example.\n" +
  "- HOW MANY entries is decided by the task's spacing and quotes, and it matters: " +
  "`cargo run -- 3 4` printing 7 is TWO arguments, args [\"3\", \"4\"] — while " +
  "`cargo run -- \"3 4 + 2 *\"` printing 14 is ONE argument, args [\"3 4 + 2 *\"]. Quotes that " +
  "DELIMIT a value are not part of it: no surrounding quotes in the entries or in expect.\n" +
  "- cargo_test=true ONLY if the task explicitly requires a unit test to pass.\n" +
  "- checkable=false if the task has no machine-checkable build/run/test criterion. In particular, " +
  "if the task is NOT about a Rust program — it only asks for a chat reply, an explanation, or " +
  "plain text (e.g. \"reply with the word pong\") — return checkable=false. The acceptance is the " +
  "reply itself, which is NOT a build/run/test; do NOT invent a `cargo run` or an argument for it.\n\n" +
  "Task:\n"

/** The gate is opt-OUT: the failure it prevents is silent, and an unverified run looks
  * exactly like a verified one until somebody reads the output. */
def gateEnabled(): Boolean =
  env("NADIA_VERIFY") match
    case Some(v) => !(v == "0" || v == "off" || v == "false" || v == "no")
    case None    => true

/** Two, like the other two implementations: a model that has not converged in two rounds is
  * not converging, and each round is a full turn somebody is waiting for. */
def gateRounds(): Int =
  env("NADIA_VERIFY_ROUNDS") match
    case Some(v) => v.toIntOption.getOrElse(2)
    case None    => 2

/** Shell-quote, so a model-supplied string is inert inside the derived command. */
def shquote(s: String): String = "'" + s.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"

/** Strip ONE symmetric pair of delimiting quotes; leave one-sided or internal quotes alone,
  * because there the quote is data (`he said "hi"`, `"a" + "b"`). */
def unquote(s: String): String =
  val t = s.trim()
  def strip(q: String): Option[String] =
    if t.length >= 2 && t.startsWith(q) && t.endsWith(q) then
      val inner = t.substring(1, t.length - 1)
      if inner.contains(q) then None else Some(inner)
    else None
  strip("\"") match
    case Some(v) => v
    case None =>
      strip("'") match
        case Some(v) => v
        case None    => t

/** Split a command line the way a shell does — whitespace separates, quotes group. No escapes
  * and no expansion: this reads the argument list a TASK wrote. */
def shellLex(s: String): List[String] =
  var out = List[String]()
  var cur = ""
  var quote = ""
  var has = false
  for c <- s.toCharArray.toList do
    val ch = c.toString
    if quote != "" then
      if ch == quote then quote = "" else cur = cur + ch
    else if ch == "\"" || ch == "'" then
      quote = ch
      has = true
    // A task is MARKDOWN: backticks fence the example, they are not part of an argument. Measured
    // 2026-08-13 by the contract corpus on the matrix's own rpn prompt. Twin of the Rust and Scala
    // arms; the corpus is what keeps the three honest.
    else if ch == " " || ch == "\t" || ch == "`" then
      if has || cur != "" then
        out = out :+ cur
        cur = ""
        has = false
    else cur = cur + ch
  if has || cur != "" then out = out :+ cur
  out

/** Does the task actually STATE this expected output?
  *
  * A task can say what a program must do without saying what it prints — `wordcount` reads a data
  * file and prints the top three words, and the answer is in the file, not in the task. Asked to
  * formalize that, the model invents one: measured 2026-08-05 it answered `a 3 / c 2 / d 2`, three
  * lines that appear nowhere, and the check then demanded them. No correct program can pass that
  * (0/4 before the guard, 3/3 after). The task is the source of truth; the model may point at it. */
def taskStates(task: String, expect: String): Boolean =
  def squeeze(x: String): String =
    x.split(" ").flatMap(w => w.split("\n")).filter(w => w != "").mkString(" ").toLowerCase
  val e = squeeze(expect)
  // An empty expectation ("prints nothing") is real and cannot be looked up — not by this route.
  e != "" && squeeze(task).contains(e)

/** The arity of an example, taken from the TASK rather than from the model. The model is good at
  * "what should this print" and bad at shell lexing — and lexing is the one part we can do
  * exactly, because the task wrote the list with its quotes. Measured 2026-08-04 in BOTH
  * directions: asked for one string it merged `cargo run -- 3 4`; asked for a list it split
  * `cargo run -- "3 4 + 2 *"` into five. `None` when the task states no such example. */
def taskArgvFor(task: String, joined: String): Option[List[String]] =
  val want = joined.split(" ").filter(w => w != "")
  if want.isEmpty then None
  else
    val marker = "cargo run --"
    val i = task.indexOf(marker)
    if i < 0 then None
    else
      val rest = task.substring(i + marker.length)
      val line = rest.split("\n").headOption.getOrElse(rest)
      val lexed = shellLex(line)
      var found: Option[List[String]] = None
      var n = 1
      while n <= lexed.length && found.isEmpty do
        val prefix = lexed.take(n)
        val flat = prefix.flatMap(w => w.split(" ").filter(x => x != ""))
        if flat == want then found = Some(prefix)
        n = n + 1
      found

/** A `cargo run` check that SAYS what it saw — a silent comparison leaves the repair round
  * with an empty error on exactly the mismatches that matter. */
def cargoRunFragment(arg: String, expect: String): String =
  cargoRunFragmentArgs(List(arg), expect)

/** The same check for a program invoked with SEVERAL arguments. Arity is part of the criterion
  * and the single-string form could not express it: measured end-to-end 2026-08-04, "cargo run
  * -- 3 4 must print 7" derived one literal `'3 4'`, which a correct two-argument program
  * rejects — FAILED reported on work that was right. */
def cargoRunFragmentArgs(args: List[String], expect: String): String =
  val a = args.map(x => shquote(unquote(x))).mkString(" ")
  val shown = shquote(args.map(x => unquote(x)).mkString(" "))
  val e = shquote(unquote(expect))
  "{ out=$(cargo run -q -- " + a + ") && [ \"$out\" = " + e + " ] || " +
  "{ printf 'cargo run -- %s printed <%s>; expected <%s>\\n' " + shown + " \"$out\" " + e + " >&2; exit 1; }; }"

def taskMentionsCargo(task: String): Boolean =
  val p = task.toLowerCase()
  List("cargo", "rust", "crate", "src/main.rs", "src/lib.rs", ".rs").exists(k => p.contains(k))

/** A cargo check for a workspace with no manifest and a task that never mentioned Rust is one
  * the model invented. */
def isHallucinatedCargoCheck(check: String, root: String, task: String): Boolean =
  check.contains("cargo") && !exists(root + "/Cargo.toml") && !taskMentionsCargo(task)

/** The floor: a cargo project must at least build. */
def cargoFloor(root: String): Option[String] =
  if !exists(root + "/Cargo.toml") then None
  else
    val probe = exec("sh", List("-c", "grep -rqs '#\\[test\\]' src tests 2>/dev/null"),
                     ProcessOptions(cwd = Some(root)))
    if probe.exitCode == 0 then Some("cargo build -q && cargo test -q") else Some("cargo build -q")

/** Ask the model to formalize the task, then BUILD the command from its structured answer —
  * the model supplies values, never shell. `None` when nothing is machine-checkable. */
def deriveCheck(endpoint: AgentEndpoint, model: String, task: String): Option[String] =
  val r = runAgent(endpoint, model, "You reply with JSON and nothing else.", derivePrompt + task,
                   List(), RunOptions(temperature = 0.0, maxSteps = 1))
  val text = r.text
  val a = text.indexOf("{")
  val b = text.lastIndexOf("}")
  if a < 0 || b < a then None
  else
    val j = jsonValue(text.substring(a, b + 1))
    if !j.get("checkable").asBool then None
    else
      val head = List("cargo build -q")
      val withTest = if j.get("cargo_test").asBool then head :+ "cargo test -q" else head
      // `args` carries the arity the single string could not; `arg` stays valid as ONE argument.
      val runs = j.get("run").asList.map { r =>
        val listed = r.get("args").asList.map(v => v.asString).filter(x => x != "")
        val argv = if listed.nonEmpty then listed
                   else if r.get("arg").asString != "" then List(r.get("arg").asString)
                   else List()
        val exp = r.get("expect").asString
        // An expectation the task never states is the model inventing the answer.
        if argv.isEmpty || !taskStates(task, exp) then ""
        else
          // Arity comes from the task's own punctuation when the task states the example.
          val joined = argv.map(x => unquote(x)).mkString(" ")
          cargoRunFragmentArgs(taskArgvFor(task, joined).getOrElse(argv), r.get("expect").asString)
      }.filter(f => f != "")
      Some((withTest ++ runs).mkString(" && "))

/** Run the check. Returns the exit status and the tail of what it printed — the second half
  * IS the repair prompt, so cargo's progress noise goes and the diagnosis stays. */
def runCheck(cmd: String, root: String): (Boolean, String) =
  // `exec` takes (program, args, options) and its cwd is an Option — the shell is explicit here
  // because the derived check is a shell expression (`&&`, `$( )`, a printf fallback), not a
  // program with arguments.
  val r = exec("sh", List("-c", cmd), ProcessOptions(cwd = Some(root)))
  val all = r.stdout + r.stderr
  val kept = all.split("\n").toList.filter { l =>
    val t = l.trim()
    !t.startsWith("Compiling") && !t.startsWith("Finished") && !t.startsWith("Running")
  }
  val tail = if kept.length > 40 then kept.drop(kept.length - 40) else kept
  (r.exitCode == 0, tail.mkString("\n"))

/** The single child directory holding a Cargo.toml when the root holds none. Ambiguity gives
  * None: a hint that names the wrong directory is worse than no hint. */
def misplacedProject(root: String): Option[String] =
  // `entriesOf` rather than `listDir`: this runs on the FAILURE path, where the workspace may be
  // exactly what went wrong (an agent that deleted its own root — rozum BUG-017), and a
  // diagnostic that throws while diagnosing is worse than no diagnostic. The guard used to be a
  // remembered `isDir` here, which is how it came to be missing in the first place.
  if exists(root + "/Cargo.toml") then None
  else
    val kids = entriesOf(root).filter(name => exists(root + "/" + name + "/Cargo.toml"))
    if kids.length == 1 then Some(kids.head) else None

/** What a failed check sends back: the command, what it printed, and — when it applies — the
  * one diagnosis a model cannot reach by reading the error. */
def repairPrompt(check: String, output: String, root: String): String =
  val base =
    "The acceptance check for this task FAILED. This is the real output, not a summary.\n\n" +
    "$ " + check + "\n" + output + "\n\n" +
    "Fix the cause and make the check pass. Do not explain the failure instead of fixing it, " +
    "and do not report success until you have run the check yourself and read what it printed."
  misplacedProject(root) match
    case Some(dir) =>
      base + "\n\nNOTE: the check runs in the workspace ROOT, and there is no Cargo.toml there — " +
      "the project is in `" + dir + "/`. `cargo new " + dir + "` created a subdirectory; the " +
      "project has to be in the root itself. Move it up or recreate it with `cargo init` in the " +
      "root, then run the check again."
    case None => base
```
